  "honest_commentary": [
    "#1 finding (clean edge, ROBUST): TIGHTER STOP wins. orig_1.0 stops (= original price risk in $) consistently beat orig_2.0 stops across exit policies. The demand zone either holds cleanly or it does not \u2014 extra room above the original stop adds losses without saving wins. Net effect: ~+15-25R adjusted at half size.",
    "#2 finding (clean edge, ROBUST): TRAIL/BE_dyn exits dominate fixed targets in honest accounting. fixR_3 captures most wins (+17R), fixR_5 gives back too many (+3.5R), fixR_7..20 are net negative because too few hit. Trail-50%-arm-at-5R and BE@2-dyn-20 harvest the fat tail without missing too many \u2014 both land in the +25-40R adjusted range at half size.",
    "#3 finding (real but smaller edge): Asymmetric (partial + runner BE) beats baseline by 5-15R adjusted. Lower variance, fewer total dollars at risk per trade, but lower upside than pure trail. Worth considering for FundedNext streak constraints (capping the lock + small partial reduces drawdown shocks).",
    "#4 finding (NEGATIVE): entry timing (demand_low vs mid vs high vs confirmation) barely shifts results. Within \u00b15R adjusted across the four variants. Once you commit to re-entering, WHERE inside the zone is noise.",
    "#5 finding (NEGATIVE): time_decay sizing and short wait windows do NOT help. Losers are losers regardless of how patient you are; cap max wait to 100 bars only to limit capital tie-up, not for edge. multi_level demand was treated as = demand_low because the dataset only exposes one nearest zone \u2014 flagged for honesty.",
    "#6 caveat (capital normalization is critical): without it, \"orig_1.0 + half\" looks worse than \"orig_2.0 + half\" in raw sumR_adjusted, because half-size on a smaller-$-risk stop is half capital deployed. When normalized to 1\u00d7 original $-risk per trade, orig_1.0 wins clearly. ALWAYS COMPARE AT EQUAL $-RISK.",
    "#7 caveat (path-order assumptions): trail variants showing 0 explicit \"winners\" rely on bar-level path ordering. Real intrabar tick sequence may differ. Expect live performance to differ from backtest by 10-20%. The robust ordering is: trail beats BE_dyn beats asym beats fixR \u2014 the direction holds, magnitudes may shift.",
    "#8 caveat (sample bias and over-fit): I tested ~135 variants on 69 trading instances. The single best has some look-ahead-fit risk. A safer pick: orig_1.0 + BE_2_dyn_20 + half = +32.88R adjusted (similar story, simpler rule, less over-fit). Or for asymmetric story: orig_1.5 + asym_5_2_20 + half = +30.12R.",
    "#9 limitation: this re-entry layer only fires on already-known losers. Live deployment requires re-entering on EVERY caminho-B trade (because we do not know in advance whether it will be a winner). Adding +0.5x size on every re-entry across the full 117-trade sample = different P&L profile than just rescuing the 78 losers. The Caminho B winner subset (25 monumental + others) may have different demand-zone re-entry behavior. Recommend: run this same variant on the 25 winners and 14 other trades to get full picture before deploying.",
    "#10 ranking robustness: variants that consistently land top-15 across raw, normalized, AND half-only rankings are most trustworthy. By that test: orig_1.0 + trail_5_0.5 (any size), orig_1.0 + BE_2_dyn_20, orig_1.5 + trail_3_0.5, orig_2.0 + trail_3_0.5, and orig_2.0 + BE_2_dyn_20 are the safest recommendations."
  ]
